1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:02,466
Islamabad, Pakistan.

2
00:00:02,506 --> 00:00:08,350
Through a back-channel, Iran and the
United States sat down together.

3
00:00:08,390 --> 00:00:14,406
A draft 72-hour conditional pause
arrived — Israel was not in the room.

4
00:00:14,446 --> 00:00:19,147
When Iran's delegate said
Fordow was not on the agenda,

5
00:00:19,187 --> 00:00:22,377
the silence in the room grew heavier.

6
00:00:24,577 --> 00:00:29,430
Dr. Leila Shirazi confirmed the
Fordow signal for the third time.

7
00:00:29,470 --> 00:00:34,300
Five days after the strike, a
facility rated “fully neutralized”

8
00:00:34,340 --> 00:00:36,507
was pulsing again, regularly.

9
00:00:36,547 --> 00:00:40,844
Eighty meters underground,
after two bunker-buster hits —

10
00:00:40,884 --> 00:00:43,699
something was still alive down there.

11
00:00:45,899 --> 00:00:49,719
The IAEA director-general
raised the possibility

12
00:00:49,759 --> 00:00:53,338
that part of Fordow's
structure had survived.

13
00:00:53,378 --> 00:00:57,847
Hours later, the Israeli PM's
office issued a statement:

14
00:00:57,887 --> 00:01:02,597
“Israel retains the right to
continue unilateral operations

15
00:01:02,637 --> 00:01:06,864
until Iran's nuclear capability
is fully eliminated.”

16
00:01:06,904 --> 00:01:10,167
The 72-hour pause had no seat for Israel.

17
00:01:12,367 --> 00:01:19,125
Under a sky finally thinning of smoke,
hundreds of thousands filled the square.

18
00:01:19,165 --> 00:01:25,493
Women without hijabs, old men with
faded flags, parents carrying children.

19
00:01:25,533 --> 00:01:30,226
A Nowruz song rose three days
before spring's new year.

20
00:01:30,266 --> 00:01:35,171
The IRGC held back — one Basij
member set down his baton.

21
00:01:37,371 --> 00:01:41,596
The U.S. Navy's mine
countermeasures force declared the

22
00:01:41,636 --> 00:01:45,705
northern shipping lane of
the Strait of Hormuz clear.

23
00:01:45,745 --> 00:01:50,928
Limited tanker traffic resumed; oil
eased slightly to $138 a barrel.

24
00:01:50,968 --> 00:01:54,578
The throat through which
a fifth of the world's

25
00:01:54,618 --> 00:01:58,113
oil passes drew its first
breath in six days.

26
00:02:00,313 --> 00:02:02,775
The air conditioning was broken.

27
00:02:02,815 --> 00:02:05,042
Twelve people sweated anyway.

28
00:02:05,082 --> 00:02:11,496
The U.S. deputy assistant secretary sat
across from Iran's IRGC political liaison,

29
00:02:11,536 --> 00:02:16,770
with a Pakistani mediator between
them who officially wasn't there.

30
00:02:16,810 --> 00:02:20,210
“Seventy-two hours,”
the American side said.

31
00:02:20,250 --> 00:02:23,135
“In exchange: no new mines in Hormuz,

32
00:02:23,175 --> 00:02:27,403
no new ballistic launches,
and IAEA access to Fordow.”

33
00:02:27,443 --> 00:02:31,390
The Iranian liaison said
nothing for a long moment.

34
00:02:31,430 --> 00:02:35,378
“Fordow,” he finally said,
“is not on this agenda.”

35
00:02:35,418 --> 00:02:38,540
“There's a signal coming out of Fordow,”

36
00:02:38,580 --> 00:02:42,492
the American side answered
— quietly, but clearly.

37
00:02:42,532 --> 00:02:47,809
“Without that, there's nothing to
discuss beyond seventy-two hours.”

38
00:02:47,849 --> 00:02:52,150
Wars begin with gunfire, but
their real weight is often

39
00:02:52,190 --> 00:02:56,215
decided in rooms where no
one says anything at all.

40
00:02:58,415 --> 00:03:02,107
Dr. Leila Shirazi checked
the signal three times.

41
00:03:02,147 --> 00:03:06,144
First she thought it was an
error, then interference.

42
00:03:06,184 --> 00:03:10,105
By the third check, she
couldn't deny it any longer.

43
00:03:10,145 --> 00:03:13,648
Five days after two
bunker-busters hit Fordow,

44
00:03:13,688 --> 00:03:17,037
the assessment had read
“fully neutralized.”

45
00:03:17,077 --> 00:03:21,807
But the signal held a regular
rhythm inside its irregularity —

46
00:03:21,847 --> 00:03:24,654
a truly dead system couldn't do that.

47
00:03:24,694 --> 00:03:26,863
She began writing the report.

48
00:03:26,903 --> 00:03:30,962
The last transmission from
a source codenamed ZARRIN,

49
00:03:31,002 --> 00:03:33,824
routed through the Islamabad channel,

50
00:03:33,864 --> 00:03:39,470
had arrived with seven coordinates for
a surviving sublevel and one line:

51
00:03:39,510 --> 00:03:41,945
“This is the last transmission.”

52
00:03:41,985 --> 00:03:44,687
The source's safety was in question.

53
00:03:44,727 --> 00:03:47,921
Seven months ago she'd written that only a

54
00:03:47,961 --> 00:03:51,695
kinetic intervention could
change the trajectory.

55
00:03:51,735 --> 00:03:53,980
They'd made that intervention.

56
00:03:54,020 --> 00:03:58,550
If it hadn't been enough — what
came next, she didn't write.

57
00:03:58,590 --> 00:04:00,038
Someone else would.

58
00:04:02,238 --> 00:04:06,343
Israel's prime minister
received Washington's 72-hour

59
00:04:06,383 --> 00:04:10,019
proposal as a notification,
not a consultation.

60
00:04:10,059 --> 00:04:12,286
He knew the difference well —

61
00:04:12,326 --> 00:04:18,382
and this notification meant Israel had
been left out of the Islamabad channel.

62
00:04:18,422 --> 00:04:22,641
“Fordow is alive,” he told
his chief of staff. “It is.”

63
00:04:22,681 --> 00:04:25,274
“Then our mission isn't finished.”

64
00:04:25,314 --> 00:04:26,281
That was all.

65
00:04:26,321 --> 00:04:31,059
The short sentence became an
official statement within hours:

66
00:04:31,099 --> 00:04:35,681
“Israel retains the right to
continue unilateral operations

67
00:04:35,721 --> 00:04:39,832
until Iran's nuclear capability
is fully eliminated.”

68
00:04:39,872 --> 00:04:42,233
The world read it as a warning.

69
00:04:42,273 --> 00:04:47,926
It wasn't a warning — it was the
announcement of a decision already made.

70
00:04:50,126 --> 00:04:56,260
Captain Marcus Webb was alone on the bridge
when the mine-clearance report came in.

71
00:04:56,300 --> 00:05:01,889
One of the narrowest throats on earth,
carrying a fifth of the world's oil,

72
00:05:01,929 --> 00:05:04,741
was opening again after six days shut.

73
00:05:04,781 --> 00:05:10,916
Sarah Callahan came up without her camera
— for the first time since the war began.

74
00:05:10,956 --> 00:05:13,148
“The strait's open,” she said.

75
00:05:13,188 --> 00:05:14,561
“Limited,” he said.

76
00:05:14,601 --> 00:05:16,123
“Northern lane only.”

77
00:05:16,163 --> 00:05:17,686
“Still open, though.”

78
00:05:17,726 --> 00:05:19,694
He nodded without speaking.

79
00:05:19,734 --> 00:05:23,563
On the horizon, the silhouette
of a tanker appeared.

80
00:05:23,603 --> 00:05:26,018
“I heard about Fordow,” she said.

81
00:05:26,058 --> 00:05:28,919
“It's not over, is it.” “Probably not.”

82
00:05:28,959 --> 00:05:33,903
He knew that the strait opening
today didn't mean the war was over.

83
00:05:33,943 --> 00:05:37,068
Not while Fordow's signal was still alive.

84
00:05:39,268 --> 00:05:44,768
Captain Kim Junho sat with the phone
in his hand for twenty minutes.

85
00:05:44,808 --> 00:05:47,212
Nine times he'd tried to call.

86
00:05:47,252 --> 00:05:51,204
Nine times he'd stopped
just short of the button.

87
00:05:51,244 --> 00:05:55,970
Six days into the war, he hadn't
called his parents once —

88
00:05:56,010 --> 00:06:00,654
afraid that his mother's voice
would make all of it real.

89
00:06:00,694 --> 00:06:02,121
He finally dialed.

90
00:06:02,161 --> 00:06:06,764
She picked up on the second
ring, past midnight in Seoul.

91
00:06:06,804 --> 00:06:08,312
She'd been waiting.

92
00:06:08,352 --> 00:06:08,964
“Junho.”

93
00:06:09,004 --> 00:06:11,082
Two syllables were enough.

94
00:06:11,122 --> 00:06:15,971
“I'm alive,” he said, his voice
steadier than he'd expected.

95
00:06:16,011 --> 00:06:17,518
“I know,” she said.

96
00:06:17,558 --> 00:06:21,429
“We've been watching the
news. Your father too.”

97
00:06:21,469 --> 00:06:27,458
A short silence, then her voice dropped
to almost a whisper. “Sleep well.”

98
00:06:27,498 --> 00:06:33,894
She asked nothing else — not when he'd
come home, not whether it was dangerous.

99
00:06:33,934 --> 00:06:39,637
Not asking was how she said: I already
know everything, and I'll wait.

100
00:06:41,837 --> 00:06:44,236
Under a sky thinning of smoke,

101
00:06:44,276 --> 00:06:48,950
Soraya Tahir stood in the middle
of Azadi Square and sang.

102
00:06:48,990 --> 00:06:53,730
Not a war song — an old Persian
song for the end of winter,

103
00:06:53,770 --> 00:06:58,997
for flowers opening, one the
revolution had never managed to ban.

104
00:06:59,037 --> 00:07:02,814
Women without hijabs, old
men with faded flags,

105
00:07:02,855 --> 00:07:06,632
parents holding children
joined in, one by one.

106
00:07:06,672 --> 00:07:10,731
The Basij came out onto
Vali-e Asr Avenue that day.

107
00:07:10,771 --> 00:07:13,384
But they didn't move to crush it.

108
00:07:13,424 --> 00:07:18,367
One of them set his baton down
on the pavement and sang along.

109
00:07:18,407 --> 00:07:21,742
Someone laid a spring flower on top of it.

110
00:07:21,782 --> 00:07:27,529
Two days later, that image would be
shared more than any other on earth.

111
00:07:27,569 --> 00:07:29,780
The war had broken the city.

112
00:07:29,820 --> 00:07:34,160
But spring didn't stop —
spring is older than the war.

113
00:07:36,360 --> 00:07:43,035
Professor Azad Moradi opened the last blank
page of a notebook he'd bought in 1973.

114
00:07:43,075 --> 00:07:47,566
Yesterday's song from the
square still rang in his ears.

115
00:07:47,606 --> 00:07:54,120
The smoke outside was thinner than yesterday's
— the effect of the 72-hour pause.

116
00:07:54,160 --> 00:07:58,117
Whether that meant peace
or simply a held breath,

117
00:07:58,157 --> 00:08:03,909
fifty-three years of teaching philosophy
hadn't taught him how to know.

118
00:08:03,949 --> 00:08:06,453
He picked up his pen and wrote:

119
00:08:06,493 --> 00:08:10,883
“Fifty-three years ago, on
this notebook's first page,

120
00:08:10,923 --> 00:08:15,560
I wrote — truth is uncomfortable,
but a lie hurts longer.

121
00:08:15,600 --> 00:08:21,062
Today, on the last page, I write
this: freedom is uncomfortable too.

122
00:08:21,102 --> 00:08:23,408
But repression hurts longer.”

123
00:08:23,448 --> 00:08:25,269
He closed the notebook.

124
00:08:25,309 --> 00:08:28,788
From the courtyard, the
singing rose again.

125
00:08:30,988 --> 00:08:33,344
Fordow's signal changed again.

126
00:08:33,384 --> 00:08:38,362
Its strength was rising — a last
surge as residual power died,

127
00:08:38,402 --> 00:08:42,004
a restart by Iranian
technicians underground,

128
00:08:42,044 --> 00:08:45,323
or the third possibility she feared most.

129
00:08:45,363 --> 00:08:48,518
There was no way to know which, not yet.

130
00:08:48,558 --> 00:08:51,522
IAEA access needed Iran's permission;

131
00:08:51,562 --> 00:08:55,337
Iran's permission needed
progress in the talks;

132
00:08:55,377 --> 00:08:58,341
progress needed Israel to stand down.

133
00:08:58,381 --> 00:09:04,011
She knew, in numbers, how uncertain
each link in that chain really was.

134
00:09:04,051 --> 00:09:06,008
She wrote another report.

135
00:09:06,048 --> 00:09:12,207
The final line was different from the
one she'd written seven months earlier:

136
00:09:12,247 --> 00:09:15,911
“Kinetic intervention
alone is not sufficient.

137
00:09:15,951 --> 00:09:18,147
The next step is diplomatic.

138
00:09:18,187 --> 00:09:23,019
Without it, Fordow's signal
cannot be stopped.” She filed it.

139
00:09:23,059 --> 00:09:26,293
Ending a war is harder than starting one.

140
00:09:26,333 --> 00:09:31,564
Bombs bring down buildings — but
a signal doesn't stop for a bomb.

141
00:09:31,604 --> 00:09:33,082
— End of Episode 3.

142
00:09:33,122 --> 00:09:35,118
Continued in Episode 4. —
